  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Quiet Workshop",
    "overview": "layercents is the calm corner of a maker's workshop at the end of the day: the printers are humming, the room is dark and warm, and the one thing lit up is the number that matters. The interface should feel peaceful and comfortable to sit with, elegant rather than technical, so that working out money (a task most makers dislike) becomes something people want to come back to. Dark graphite surfaces stack like print layers; gold is the light on the bench, reserved for the answer and the next action.\n\nDensity is moderate and desk-first: a two-column calculator beside the slicer, where inputs sit on the left and the answer stays in view on the right. Numbers are always tabular, aligned and unhurried. Paper is the other material: the customer quote and the report are white sheets with ink and a deeper print gold, identical on screen and in the PDF, because they leave the workshop.\n\nThe incumbent components are sober and precise (thin seams, 5px corners, dark buttons that light up gold on hover). The stated direction is **warmer and more tactile**: when components are refined, they should gain physical presence (softer corners, fuller fills, a sense of something you can press) without losing the calm. Treat that as the direction of travel, not a reason to restyle untouched screens.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Dark, warm graphite in three tonal layers; no light theme for the app itself.",
      "Gold is light, not decoration: the recommended price, the one primary action, focus and live state.",
